<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="prep-2-5">
  <title>Resources for 2/5</title>

  <objectives>
    <introduction>
      <p>For the content of this class, you will be expected to be able to do the following:</p>
    </introduction>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
          Explain the definition of an <em>equivalence class</em> of a relatio
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Explain how only equivalence relations give rise to equivalence classes.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Given an equivalence relation, determine all of its equivalence classes.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </objectives>

  <subsection xml:id="video-activity-equivalence-class-ex">
    <title>Video &amp; Activities for Equivalence Classes - Examples</title>
    <p>
      Below is a YouTube video on <em>equivalence classes</em>. It is followed by a link to a set of activities on an example that we will be working on in class. Please watch the video and do as many of the activities as you can before class.
    </p>

    <video youtube="IZzOgiYB4o4" />

    <problem>
      <title>Equivalence Class Examples</title>
      <p>
        Find the equivalence classes of the following relations.
      </p>

      <p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              Let <m>A</m> be the set of all the ways you can write the numbers 1, 2, 3, and 4 in a square.  Define the relation <m>R</m> on <m>A</m> as follows.  For each pair of squares <m>s_1,s_2 \in A</m>, we say that <m>s_1Rs_2</m> if <m>s_1</m> can be rotated so that it looks exactly like <m>s_2</m>.
              <ol>
                <li>
                  <p>
                    Write down two squares that are related to one another.
                  </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                  <p>
                    Write down two squares that are <em>not</em> related to one another.
                  </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                  <p>
                    Prove that <m>R</m> is an equivalence relation.
                  </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                  <p>
                    Find all equivalence classes of <m>R</m>.
                  </p>
                </li>
              </ol>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Let <m>A = \{ 0,1,2,3,4,5 \}</m>, and define <m>E</m> on <m>A</m> by <m>aEb</m> precisely when <m>a^2 \equiv b^2</m> (mod 6).  Do parts (a) through (d) above for <m>E</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </problem>
  </subsection>

  <subsection>
    <title>Optional Reading</title>
    <p>
      Section 7.3: pp. 387-391
    </p>
  </subsection>
</section>